8-Bit Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU) Using Verilog

Project Overview

This project implements an 8-bit Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU) using Verilog HDL. The ALU performs arithmetic and bitwise logical operations based on a 3-bit select input.

This is a beginner-friendly digital design project created to learn RTL design, combinational logic, and functional simulation.

Features

- 8-bit input operands: A and B
- 3-bit operation selector: "sel"
- 8-bit output: "result"
- Six defined operations: ADD, SUB, AND, OR, XOR, and NOT A
- Default output of zero for select codes "110" and "111"
- Testbench with 13 test cases, including boundary-value tests

Operation Table

Select ("sel")| Operation| Description
"000"| ADD| A + B
"001"| SUB| A - B
"010"| AND| Bitwise AND of A and B
"011"| OR| Bitwise OR of A and B
"100"| XOR| Bitwise XOR of A and B
"101"| NOT A| Bitwise inversion of A
"110"| Default| Output zero
"111"| Default| Output zero

Project Files

- "alu.v" — Verilog description of the ALU.
- "alu_tb.v" — Testbench used to verify the ALU.

Tools Used

- Verilog HDL
- EDA Playground
- Icarus Verilog simulator, if selected in EDA Playground

Testing

The testbench checks all eight select codes and five additional boundary-value cases.

Tested cases include:

- Addition: 5 + 3 = 8
- Subtraction: 5 - 3 = 2
- Bitwise logical operations
- Maximum 8-bit unsigned input values
- Zero inputs
- 8-bit arithmetic wraparound: 255 + 1 produces 0 because the carry beyond bit 7 is discarded

Simulation status: All 13 test cases passed in the simulation.

